          "text": "The user is asking about CUDA graphs \u2014 a CUDA feature introduced around CUDA 10 that allows capturing a series of kernel launches (and other CUDA work like memcpys, event records, etc.) into a graph structure, then replaying that graph with a single launch call. The question is: why can replaying a graph be faster than launching the same kernels eagerly (i.e., calling `cudaLaunchKernel` for each one), and what are the costs/tradeoffs?\n\nLet me think through the technical reasons carefully, because this is a technical question and I want to be accurate.\n\n**Why CUDA graph replay can be faster:**\n\n1. **CPU-side launch overhead reduction.** Each eager kernel launch involves the CPU doing work: the runtime/driver has to validate parameters, allocate/prepare the launch command, push it into a command buffer, etc. Even though launches are asynchronous, the CPU-side cost per launch is on the order of a few microseconds (historically ~5-10 \u00b5s, improved over time but still nontrivial). If you have thousands of small kernels, the CPU can become the bottleneck \u2014 the GPU finishes work faster than the CPU can submit it. A graph replay is a single API call (`cudaGraphLaunch`), so the CPU",
